1
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:09,802
Somewhere in Africa, a power deal is signed in front of the cameras.

2
00:00:10,102 --> 00:00:13,729
Minister Kerbu: Today we sign for one hundred megawatts of power for our people!

3
00:00:14,029 --> 00:00:15,927
Emson Anahct: We are ready to build, Minister.

4
00:00:16,227 --> 00:00:20,089
Everyone applauds. But who in this room will carry the risk?

5
00:00:21,839 --> 00:00:28,004
The MoU trap. A training module for ministers, cabinets, regulators, and their partners.

6
00:00:31,254 --> 00:00:35,371
Six seats at the table. Each one sees the same deal differently.

7
00:00:35,971 --> 00:00:37,977
The government, which signs.

8
00:00:38,427 --> 00:00:41,563
The ministry of finance, which stands behind it.

9
00:00:42,013 --> 00:00:44,189
The utility, which must pay.

10
00:00:44,639 --> 00:00:46,985
The developer, who holds the option.

11
00:00:47,435 --> 00:00:49,547
The lender, who arrives last.

12
00:00:49,997 --> 00:00:52,685
And the analyst, who asks the questions.

13
00:00:54,485 --> 00:00:57,024
Three signals guide this module.

14
00:00:57,524 --> 00:01:00,105
Blue: documented facts, with their source.

15
00:01:00,705 --> 00:01:03,734
Yellow: what decision-makers must examine.

16
00:01:04,334 --> 00:01:06,425
Red: what could make the deal fail.

17
00:01:08,375 --> 00:01:12,172
When you see stop, pause the video and discuss with your team.

18
00:01:14,722 --> 00:01:17,645
Part one. Fourteen contracts, no power.

19
00:01:20,495 --> 00:01:29,754
In July 2016, Nigeria's bulk electricity trader signed power purchase agreements with fourteen solar developers.

20
00:01:32,804 --> 00:01:41,487
Together, some eleven hundred and twenty-five megawatts, at eleven and a half US cents per kilowatt-hour, fixed for twenty years.

21
00:01:44,537 --> 00:01:53,006
The target was first power in eighteen months. Ten developers reportedly paid security bonds to show they meant to move quickly.

22
00:01:56,056 --> 00:02:00,493
Three years later, none of the fourteen had reached financial close.

23
00:02:03,543 --> 00:02:10,284
Solar prices had fallen, and the government now wanted to cut the tariff to around seven and a half cents.

24
00:02:13,334 --> 00:02:22,721
The put and call option agreements that lenders expected, and the partial risk guarantees behind them, were not concluded for most of the projects.

25
00:02:25,771 --> 00:02:35,200
In 2020, a specialist publication summed it up. Eleven hundred and twenty-five megawatts promised. Not one electron delivered.

26
00:02:38,150 --> 00:02:45,169
These were not memoranda of understanding. They were signed PPAs, and most developers had put money down.

27
00:02:46,069 --> 00:02:54,965
They still stopped where a great many African power MoUs stop: at the moment when a signed commitment has to become a financeable one.

28
00:02:57,515 --> 00:03:01,120
Part two. What an MoU binds, and what it does not.

29
00:03:03,970 --> 00:03:16,087
A memorandum of understanding records an intention to cooperate. On its central commitment, most MoUs bind no one. Neither side is obliged to build, to buy, or to pay.

30
00:03:18,837 --> 00:03:23,253
But the legal reading understates what an MoU does in practice.

31
00:03:23,753 --> 00:03:28,532
Exclusivity over a site or a resource, which keeps competitors out.

32
00:03:29,082 --> 00:03:34,500
An indicative tariff, which becomes the reference point for every later negotiation.

33
00:03:35,050 --> 00:03:39,296
Promises of state help, expected before anyone has priced them.

34
00:03:39,846 --> 00:03:46,374
And a public signing: a political commitment that is harder to withdraw than any clause in the document.

35
00:03:48,324 --> 00:03:50,948
Belpau: The MoU says the tariff is only indicative.

36
00:03:51,248 --> 00:03:54,384
Enilec Mok: Every negotiation after today will start from that number.

37
00:03:56,134 --> 00:03:58,907
Three stages. Three kinds of commitment.

38
00:03:59,707 --> 00:04:05,126
At the MoU stage, little binds in law. The state gives a political signal.

39
00:04:05,726 --> 00:04:12,489
At the contract stage: tariff, term and obligations, with future payments conditional on operation.

40
00:04:13,089 --> 00:04:19,745
At financial close, it is all funded, and the state's obligations are fixed for the life of the contract.

41
00:04:22,795 --> 00:04:26,187
The trap lies between the first stage and the third.

42
00:04:27,287 --> 00:04:36,119
Some commitments never close. Others do close, on terms first sketched when the project was least tested, and the government least prepared.

43
00:04:38,669 --> 00:04:45,965
An MoU, and often the first contract after it, is signed before anyone has applied either of two tests.

44
00:04:46,465 --> 00:04:49,686
The lender's test: can the project repay its debt?

45
00:04:50,236 --> 00:04:58,066
The state's test: can the power system and the public finances carry the project's obligations for twenty years or more?

46
00:05:00,266 --> 00:05:02,506
Part three. Why projects stall.

47
00:05:05,356 --> 00:05:17,239
Across African infrastructure, McKinsey estimated that fewer than ten percent of projects reach financial close. It covers all sectors, so read it as an order of magnitude.

48
00:05:20,289 --> 00:05:35,649
Outside South Africa, private power investment from 1990 to 2014 came to roughly a quarter of one year's estimated needs. The comparison is imperfect, because the needs include the grid.

49
00:05:38,699 --> 00:05:42,710
The same pattern appears at the scale of a large gas plant.

50
00:05:43,210 --> 00:05:52,404
Qua Iboe. In 2014, the World Bank approved a partial risk guarantee of up to one hundred and fifty million dollars.

51
00:05:52,954 --> 00:05:57,456
In 2017, the government approved the PPA.

52
00:05:58,006 --> 00:06:04,939
In 2018, after two extensions, the deadline to sign the guarantee agreements expired.

53
00:06:05,489 --> 00:06:09,158
By 2020, it was reported as stalled.

54
00:06:09,708 --> 00:06:15,063
And in early 2026, a project database listed it as shelved.

55
00:06:18,063 --> 00:06:33,572
No one knows how many power MoUs African governments have signed, or how many reach financial close. Databases record projects that closed. Governments often do not publish MoUs, and developers do not announce when one lapses.

56
00:06:34,672 --> 00:06:36,998
This is the denominator problem.

57
00:06:39,548 --> 00:06:43,217
Four explanations circulate. Each is partly right.

58
00:06:43,717 --> 00:06:51,163
The sponsor layer: the developer. Screen sponsors, limit exclusivity, and attach milestones and securities.

59
00:06:51,713 --> 00:07:00,097
The process layer: how the project was originated. Compete by default, and test unsolicited proposals against the plan.

60
00:07:00,647 --> 00:07:06,769
The system layer: the plan, the grid and the buyer. Fix the system before adding contracts.

61
00:07:07,319 --> 00:07:14,679
The fiscal layer: the state's capacity to support. Apply the sovereign test, and size support to fiscal space.

62
00:07:15,229 --> 00:07:20,627
They are not rivals. They are layers. The useful question is which one binds first.

63
00:07:23,477 --> 00:07:35,509
Qua Iboe had credible sponsors, and it stalled all the same. In 2018, an official told Reuters the plant would enter a system that is illiquid and insolvent.

64
00:07:38,459 --> 00:07:39,142
Stop.

65
00:07:39,642 --> 00:07:42,116
You are the Minister of Energy.

66
00:07:42,616 --> 00:07:45,987
A developer offers an MoU for a new plant.

67
00:07:46,387 --> 00:07:48,755
It is not in the least-cost plan.

68
00:07:49,155 --> 00:07:51,651
The signing could be ready next week.

69
00:07:52,051 --> 00:07:53,416
What do you do first?

70
00:08:00,616 --> 00:08:04,051
Hold your answer. First, look at who is in the room.

71
00:08:06,151 --> 00:08:12,274
Why does the trap persist, when everyone can describe it? Look at who is in the room at signature.

72
00:08:12,574 --> 00:08:17,246
Minister Kerbu: A visible answer to power shortages. And it costs us almost nothing today.

73
00:08:17,546 --> 00:08:21,684
Emson Anahct: For me, it is an option on a site. And options can be sold.

74
00:08:21,984 --> 00:08:30,475
The utility, which must pay, is often not a party. The ministry of finance is not in the room. The lenders arrive years later.

75
00:08:32,825 --> 00:08:34,894
The government: sign readily.

76
00:08:35,494 --> 00:08:37,585
The developer: collect options.

77
00:08:38,185 --> 00:08:40,318
The utility: stay passive.

78
00:08:40,918 --> 00:08:43,158
The lenders: no early filter.

79
00:08:43,758 --> 00:08:46,638
Development partners: count what is signed.

80
00:08:49,388 --> 00:08:54,935
A signature is rewarded today. Its cost is paid later, and by someone else.

81
00:08:56,035 --> 00:09:00,920
That is why countries with capable ministries also sign weak MoUs.

82
00:09:03,470 --> 00:09:06,179
Part four. Two exits, both costly.

83
00:09:08,779 --> 00:09:21,899
Exit one: the project stalls. Years of ministry time and developer spending produce no power. Sites stay locked up. A published tariff becomes a benchmark that later bidders must argue against.

84
00:09:23,649 --> 00:09:35,446
And when a government reopens terms it has signed, investors take note. In Nigeria, analysts warned it could weaken investor trust and raise the country's overall risk profile.

85
00:09:38,196 --> 00:09:49,951
Exit two: the project closes. And it can be worse. Pressure builds to give lenders whatever they require: a state guarantee, fixed capacity payments, a hard currency tariff.

86
00:09:52,701 --> 00:10:02,258
Ghana. During and after a severe supply crisis, the distribution utility signed forty-three PPAs through noncompetitive processes.

87
00:10:05,308 --> 00:10:12,177
The World Bank projected nineteen hundred megawatts of excess capacity by 2019.

88
00:10:15,227 --> 00:10:21,435
In 2019, net sector arrears stood at two point seven five billion dollars.

89
00:10:24,485 --> 00:10:32,229
And the country was reported to be paying more than five hundred million dollars a year for electricity it did not use.

90
00:10:35,279 --> 00:10:41,914
The contracts that were financed had satisfied their lenders. Taken together, they failed the state's test.

91
00:10:44,864 --> 00:10:52,331
Both exits share a cause. The deciding questions were asked too late, or by people with no authority to say no.

92
00:10:52,831 --> 00:10:54,388
Is the plant needed?

93
00:10:54,938 --> 00:10:56,986
Can the grid carry its output?

94
00:10:57,536 --> 00:10:58,944
Can the buyer pay?

95
00:10:59,494 --> 00:11:02,758
Can the state carry the backstop if the buyer cannot?

96
00:11:04,958 --> 00:11:06,793
Part five. The way out.

97
00:11:09,343 --> 00:11:15,828
A fair account takes the case for MoUs seriously. Some projects cannot be tendered at the outset.

98
00:11:16,928 --> 00:11:26,528
A large hydropower site, a cross-border interconnector, a first project in a new technology, or a small market without staff to run a tender.

99
00:11:29,078 --> 00:11:44,715
Cameroon's Nachtigal hydropower plant was developed under a joint development agreement, not, as far as public sources show, a tender. It reached financial close with a local currency tranche of unprecedented tenor for local banks.

100
00:11:47,765 --> 00:12:00,352
But within months of full commissioning in 2025, the press reported that its payment guarantee was largely drawn. A well-structured project remains exposed to a weak buyer.

101
00:12:03,702 --> 00:12:07,329
An MoU checked against the plan before signature,

102
00:12:07,879 --> 00:12:10,567
with milestones and a sunset date,

103
00:12:11,117 --> 00:12:14,551
reviewed by the utility and the ministry of finance,

104
00:12:15,101 --> 00:12:16,189
and published,

105
00:12:16,739 --> 00:12:19,982
is no longer a trap. It is a development tool.

106
00:12:23,132 --> 00:12:26,439
The trap has five costs. Power not delivered.

107
00:12:26,989 --> 00:12:28,653
Better projects blocked.

108
00:12:29,203 --> 00:12:31,805
Scarce public attention consumed.

109
00:12:32,355 --> 00:12:35,726
Credibility lost when signed terms are reopened.

110
00:12:36,276 --> 00:12:40,713
And liabilities created when trapped projects close under pressure.

111
00:12:42,913 --> 00:12:45,729
Bring the cost, and the risk bearers, forward.

112
00:12:46,229 --> 00:12:52,778
Filter at entry: no MoU outside the least-cost plan unless it has been tested against the plan.

113
00:12:53,328 --> 00:12:58,000
Price the option, with securities, milestones and sunset clauses.

114
00:12:58,550 --> 00:13:07,596
Bring the risk bearers in early: the utility and the ministry of finance review any MoU that could lead to fixed payments or state support.

115
00:13:08,146 --> 00:13:12,178
And publish a register of power MoUs and their status.

116
00:13:14,378 --> 00:13:25,173
Nigeria shows that pricing the option is not enough on its own. Securities were posted, and the projects still stalled, on the tariff, the buyer and state support.

117
00:13:26,273 --> 00:13:30,518
The trap has to be closed at every point where a project is tested.

118
00:13:33,068 --> 00:13:37,505
Hence the Sustainable Financial Close Test proposed in the book.

119
00:13:38,805 --> 00:13:47,403
A power deal closes only when both the lender's test and the state's test have been passed, and the state's answer has been made public.

120
00:13:49,703 --> 00:13:51,879
Minister Kerbu: Before we sign anything: who has reviewed it?

121
00:13:52,179 --> 00:13:55,699
Enilec Mok: Finance has. Any support is sized to our fiscal space.

122
00:13:55,999 --> 00:13:58,922
Paul Ahmak: The utility has. We can pay for what we buy.

123
00:13:59,222 --> 00:14:02,976
Belpau: It is in the least-cost plan, with milestones and a sunset date.

124
00:14:03,276 --> 00:14:04,727
Tidianie Eugom: Then we can lend against it.

125
00:14:05,027 --> 00:14:06,584
Minister Kerbu: Then we sign, and we publish it.

126
00:14:08,334 --> 00:14:11,491
Red team. What turns an MoU into a trap?

127
00:14:12,291 --> 00:14:15,321
It is signed before either test is applied.

128
00:14:15,871 --> 00:14:18,708
It costs its signatories almost nothing.

129
00:14:19,258 --> 00:14:22,351
And the risk bearers are absent when it is signed.

130
00:14:22,901 --> 00:14:27,125
Bring the risk bearers, and their tests, to the start of the process.

131
00:14:29,975 --> 00:14:33,964
Before any power MoU is signed, ask five questions.

132
00:14:34,564 --> 00:14:38,234
One. Is it in the least-cost plan, or tested against it?

133
00:14:39,034 --> 00:14:45,391
Two. Does it carry milestones, a sunset date and, where appropriate, a development security?

134
00:14:46,191 --> 00:14:50,116
Three. Have the utility and the ministry of finance reviewed it?

135
00:14:50,916 --> 00:14:55,631
Four. Which layer could block it: sponsor, process, system, or fiscal?

136
00:14:56,431 --> 00:14:59,183
Five. Will it be published in the register?

137
00:14:59,983 --> 00:15:02,244
If not, it is not ready to sign.

138
00:15:05,394 --> 00:15:08,423
An MoU costs almost nothing to sign.

139
00:15:09,323 --> 00:15:11,670
It can cost a great deal later.

140
00:15:12,770 --> 00:15:16,077
Bring the risk bearers, and their tests, to the start.
